      title: "The Trial and Death of Socrates",
      body: `## The charge

In **399 BCE**, four years after the restoration of the democracy, **Socrates**, son of Sophroniscus, a seventy-year-old Athenian citizen, was brought to trial. The formal charge, preserved by the later writer Diogenes Laertius (2.40), read:

> "Socrates is guilty of not recognizing the gods whom the city recognizes, and of introducing other new divinities; he is also guilty of corrupting the young. The penalty proposed is death." (Plain rendering)

The accusers were **Meletus**, a young poet, who brought the charge; **Anytus**, a wealthy tanner and a leading politician of the restored democracy, who had fought with Thrasybulus against the Thirty; and **Lycon**, an orator.

## Who was Socrates?

**Socrates** (469–399 BCE) wrote nothing. We know him through the works of his followers, above all **Plato** and **Xenophon**, and through the caricature in Aristophanes's *Clouds*, which you may have read in CLT 110. He was the son of a stonemason and a midwife. He served as a hoplite in the Peloponnesian War, at Potidaea, Delium, and Amphipolis, where he was noted for his courage and endurance. He was famously ugly, with a snub nose and bulging eyes, went barefoot, wore the same cloak in summer and winter, and was poor. He spent his days in the agora and the gymnasia, questioning anyone who would talk to him, about **justice**, **courage**, **piety**, and **virtue**, and showing that those who claimed to know what these things were did not. He claimed that he knew nothing, except that he knew nothing. He said that he was guided by a divine sign, his **daimonion**, an inner voice that warned him against wrong actions. And he had among his followers some of the most brilliant and ambitious young men in Athens.

## Why was he tried?

The formal charges were religious and moral. But many historians believe that the real reasons were also **political**. Among Socrates's closest associates had been **Alcibiades**, who had betrayed Athens to Sparta and Persia, and **Critias**, the leader of the Thirty Tyrants, and **Charmides**, another of the Thirty, Plato's uncle. To many Athenians who had suffered under the Thirty, Socrates seemed the teacher of the men who had destroyed the democracy. The amnesty forbade prosecution for political crimes committed before 403; a charge of impiety and corrupting the young may have been a way around it. Fifty years later, the orator **Aeschines** told an Athenian jury plainly: "You put Socrates the sophist to death because he was shown to have educated Critias, one of the Thirty who overthrew the democracy" (*Against Timarchus* 173).

Other factors probably played a part: the long-standing prejudice against intellectuals, fed by caricatures like the *Clouds*, which Plato's Socrates names as one of the sources of the prejudice against him (*Apology* 19c); the resentment of the many people Socrates had embarrassed by exposing their ignorance; and his apparent contempt for the democracy, whose practice of choosing officials by lot he criticized.

## The trial

The trial was held before a jury of **501** citizens. Socrates's speech in his own defense survives in two versions, by Plato and by Xenophon. Plato's *Apology*, which you may have read in part in GRK 102, presents a Socrates who refuses to beg for mercy or to bring his weeping children into court, as defendants commonly did; who insists that he has spent his life in obedience to the god of Delphi, examining himself and others; and who declares that "the unexamined life is not worth living for a human being" (38a). He was found guilty by a vote of about **280 to 220**.

Under Athenian law, the prosecution and the defense each proposed a penalty, and the jury chose between them. Meletus proposed death. Socrates proposed that he be given free meals in the Prytaneum, the honor given to Olympic victors and benefactors of the city, since he had done Athens nothing but good; then, at his friends' urging, a modest fine. The jury voted for death, by a larger majority than had voted to convict.

## The death

The execution was delayed for a month, because during the annual sacred mission to the island of Delos no executions could take place. Socrates spent the time in prison, talking with his friends. In Plato's *Crito*, his old friend Crito urges him to escape, since the jailers can be bribed and friends abroad would welcome him; Socrates refuses, arguing that he must not do wrong in return for wrong, and must obey the laws of the city that raised him, even when they are applied unjustly. In the *Phaedo*, on his last day, he discusses the immortality of the soul with his friends, and then, at sunset, calmly drinks the cup of **hemlock**. His last words, as you may remember from GRK 102, were a request to pay a rooster to Asclepius. Phaedo ends: "Such was the end of our friend, a man who was, as we would say, of all those we knew in his time the best, and also the wisest and most just" (118a).

## The end of an age

The death of Socrates is the end of this course, and in a sense the end of an age. It came at the close of a century that had seen the defeat of Persia, the rise and fall of the Athenian empire, the creation of the democracy, the building of the Parthenon, the tragedies of Aeschylus, Sophocles, and Euripides, the histories of Herodotus and Thucydides, and a war that destroyed it all. It produced Plato, who turned from politics to philosophy because of what he had seen, and whose dialogues would make Socrates the founding figure of Western philosophy. Thucydides wrote that his history was meant to be "a possession for all time" (**κτῆμα ἐς αἰεί**, 1.22), for those who wished to understand the past and the future, which, human nature being what it is, would resemble it. The history of Greece from the Dark Age to the death of Socrates has been exactly that.

> **Pause and consider.** Aeschines said Athens executed Socrates because he had taught Critias; Plato said it was for his questioning. Can a democracy be right to fear the teachers of its enemies, or was Socrates's death a crime of the city against itself?`,
